Here's a simple LDPC (Low-Density Parity-Check) encoder implemented in SystemVerilog for an FPGA implementation:

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in,
    output wire data_out
);

reg [7:0] data_reg;
reg [3:0] parity_reg;

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        data_reg <= 8'b00000000;
        parity_reg <= 4'b0000;
    end
    else
    begin
        data_reg <= data_in;
        parity_reg <= parity_calc(data_reg);
        data_out <= data_reg[7:4];
    end
end

function [4:0] parity_calc(data);
    // Simple parity calculation example
    return data[3:0] ^ data[4:7];
endfunction :parity_calc

endmodule :ldpc_encoder
```

This module implements a 4x8 LDPC encoder with a parity check bit generated using a simple XOR operation on the data bits. The `ldpc_encoder` module has an input `data_in` that represents the data to be encoded and an output `data_out` that represents the encoded data. The module also has a clock input `clk` and a reset input `reset`.

The module uses two registers, `data_reg` and `parity_reg`, to store the input data and the calculated parity bit, respectively. The `always` block is used to update the registers at every clock cycle or when the reset signal is asserted.

The `parity_calc` function calculates the parity bit using a simple XOR operation on the data bits. The function takes the input data as an 8-bit vector and returns a 4-bit vector representing the parity bit.

