```javascript
function smoothLinearMap(x) {
  const smoothFactor = 0.5; // Adjust this value to control the smoothness
  const linearRangeStart = 0.25; // Adjust this value to control the position of the linear range
  const linearRangeEnd = 0.75; // Adjust this value to control the position of the linear range

  if (x <= linearRangeStart) {
    return x * smoothFactor;
  } else if (x >= linearRangeEnd) {
    return (1 - x) * smoothFactor;
  } else {
    return x;
  }
}
```

This function first smooths out the edges by multiplying the input value by `smoothFactor`. Then, it introduces a linear range in the middle by returning the input value unchanged within the range `[linearRangeStart, linearRangeEnd]`. Finally, it smooths out the edges again by multiplying the output value by `smoothFactor`.

You can adjust the `smoothFactor`, `linearRangeStart`, and `linearRangeEnd` values to control the smoothness and position of the linear range.

Here's a JavaScript function that takes a string variable in dot notation and returns all entries in an object where the nested key value matches the string key:

```javascript
function getObjectEntries(obj, key) {
  const keys = key.split(".");
  let currentObj = obj;

  for (const key of keys) {
    if (!currentObj[key]) {
      return [];
    }
    currentObj = currentObj[key];
  }

  return Object.entries(currentObj);
}
```

You can use this function like this:

